==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|lapidarie}}, from {{der|en|fro|lapidaire}}, from {{der|en|la|lapidārius|t=of stones}} (later used as a noun ‘stone-cutter’), from {{m|la|lapis|t=stone}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈlæpɪdəɹi/}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-lapidary.wav |Audio (UK)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# A person who [[cut]]s, [[polish]]es, [[engrave]]s, or [[deal]]s in [[gem]]s.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2013|author=Peter G. Read|title=Gemmology|page=289|publisher=Elsevier
|passage=In the very early days of gemstone fashioning, a polisher or '''lapidary''' would cut and polish both diamonds and other gemstones.}}
# The [[field]] in which such a person works, a subfield of [[gemology]].
# The [[process]] of such work. {{rfex|en}}
# An [[expert]] in gems or precious stones; a [[connoisseur]] of lapidary work.
# {{lb|en|archaic}} A [[treatise]] on precious stones.

